The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (unlikely(copied < len && !PageUptodate(page))) {
    copied = 0;
    goto out;
```
Reason:
This code checks if the amount of data copied is less than the intended length and if the page is not marked as uptodate. If both conditions are true, it sets `copied` to 0 and exits the function without marking the page as uptodate, even if the write operation was successful. This oversight prevents the page from being correctly updated, leading to inconsistencies in the file system.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!PageUptodate(page)) {
    if (unlikely(copied < len)) {
        copied = 0;
        goto out;
    } else if (len == PAGE_SIZE) {
        SetPageUptodate(page);
    }
}
```
Reason:
This modified logic first checks if the page is not uptodate. If the copied data is less than the intended length, it still sets `copied` to 0 and exits. However, if the length of the write operation equals the page size, it now correctly marks the page as uptodate using `SetPageUptodate(page)`. This ensures that successful writes are reflected in the page's state, resolving the inconsistency issue.

